' The `net` spellings `tcp` deliberately does NOT carry (plan-110-B).
'
' `readText`/`writeText` are gone: `write` takes a String as an overload, and
' `read` returns bytes only, because a stream read stops wherever the network
' divided the data and that need not be a character boundary. `connectTcp` and
' `listenTcp` are gone too — the package name already says TCP.
'
' These are unknown-member errors, which the resolver reports before type
' checking runs, so they get their own fixture; the arity and argument-type
' diagnostics live in `func_tcp_invalid`.
IMPORT tcp

FUNC main AS Integer
  RES client = tcp::connect("127.0.0.1", 9931)

  LET goneReadText = tcp::readText(client, 16)
  tcp::writeText(client, "x")
  RES goneConnectTcp = tcp::connectTcp("127.0.0.1", 9931)
  RES goneListenTcp = tcp::listenTcp("127.0.0.1", 0)

  RETURN 0
END FUNC
